WEBVTT

00:00:02.459 --> 00:00:09.109
<v Sarah Okafor>Morning, everyone. Thursday the 2nd of April. Can you hear me all right? Teams reckons my microphone's somewhere else.</v>

00:00:10.599 --> 00:00:18.649
<v Aisha Begum>Yeah, you're there. I was just saying, weekend plans are looking a bit soggy. Supposed to be going walking, but that weather forecast...</v>

00:00:19.100 --> 00:00:22.949
<v David Whitfield>Perfect weather for looking at a hill through a pub window.</v>

00:00:24.839 --> 00:00:32.189
<v David Whitfield>And we've got the charity bake sale on Friday. My contribution's already made. Whether it qualifies as cake is another matter.</v>

00:00:33.429 --> 00:00:37.630
<v Hannah Price>As long as we don't need a hardness test before cutting it.</v>

00:00:38.090 --> 00:00:41.939
<v Sarah Okafor>Right, before this becomes a quality incident. What's already closed out?</v>

00:00:43.789 --> 00:00:50.090
<v Fiona Doyle>I tested the cooling tower yesterday and it's all fine now. That's finished, nothing outstanding from that test.</v>

00:00:51.100 --> 00:00:53.899
<v David Whitfield>Lovely. One bit of kit behaving itself, then.</v>

00:00:55.439 --> 00:01:01.039
<v Hannah Price>And I finished the repair on the line 2 robot last night. That's done as well.</v>

00:01:01.670 --> 00:01:05.519
<v Sarah Okafor>Good, both closed. Not rolling either of those into today's work.</v>

00:01:07.390 --> 00:01:18.590
<v Hannah Price>Just an observation as well: saw the pressure on the trucks dip for a few minutes. It came back on its own. No repair request from me, just saying what I saw.</v>

00:01:18.909 --> 00:01:21.010
<v Sarah Okafor>Understood. Observation, not a new job.</v>

00:01:22.689 --> 00:01:35.640
<v Priya Raman>Similar sort of thing here, though I'm not saying it's connected. Saw the pressure on the stretch wrapper dip for a few minutes; it came back on its own. Nothing I'm proposing off the back of that.</v>

00:01:36.140 --> 00:01:41.739
<v David Whitfield>Yeah, two dips doesn't automatically mean the same fault. Easy to join dots that aren't there.</v>

00:01:43.299 --> 00:01:49.950
<v Tomasz Nowak>Um, my blocker hasn't changed. I can't get to the Fire Alarm Panel until production gives us a window.</v>

00:01:51.060 --> 00:01:55.609
<v Sarah Okafor>Right, that stays blocked. We're not calling that a fresh commitment for today.</v>

00:01:56.920 --> 00:02:07.420
<v Sarah Okafor>Tomasz Nowak, can you go to Northfield Substation to pick up the replacement parts for the line 3 barrel today at 2pm? Not an emergency but don't let it slip.</v>

00:02:08.599 --> 00:02:14.900
<v Tomasz Nowak>Sorry, no, I can't commit to going off site at that time. Don't leave the collection with me.</v>

00:02:17.150 --> 00:02:29.750
<v Sarah Okafor>Okay, handing that to PR instead. Can you go to Northfield Substation to pick up the replacement parts for the line 3 barrel today at 2pm? Same thing: not an emergency but don't let it slip.</v>

00:02:30.539 --> 00:02:36.139
<v Priya Raman>Yeah, I'll take that. Northfield Substation today at 2pm, replacement parts for the line 3 barrel.</v>

00:02:36.939 --> 00:02:41.139
<v Fiona Doyle>That's the collection only, isn't it? We're not bundling fitting into that?</v>

00:02:42.060 --> 00:02:46.960
<v Priya Raman>Correct, collection only. I'm not committing to fitting them as part of that run.</v>

00:02:48.280 --> 00:02:55.280
<v Sarah Okafor>PR, also, can you check the guarding on the Line 2 Safety Guard Interlock first thing tomorrow? Top priority please.</v>

00:02:56.050 --> 00:03:02.699
<v Priya Raman>Yes, I'll check the guarding on the Line 2 Safety Guard Interlock first thing tomorrow. That's my top priority.</v>

00:03:04.659 --> 00:03:08.509
<v David Whitfield>Sorry, talking over you there—guarding check, rather than another robot repair?</v>

00:03:10.180 --> 00:03:16.129
<v Priya Raman>Yeah, the guarding on the Line 2 Safety Guard Interlock. Separate from the repair that's already finished.</v>

00:03:17.889 --> 00:03:24.189
<v Sarah Okafor>Hannah, can you send the shift handover report round at half two? It matters but it's not urgent.</v>

00:03:24.740 --> 00:03:30.689
<v Hannah Price>Yep, I'll send the shift handover report round at half two. Important, but not an urgent one.</v>

00:03:33.080 --> 00:03:38.680
<v Hannah Price>And yes, I heard half two, not half twelve. My audio did a little wobble then.</v>

00:03:39.939 --> 00:03:44.139
<v Sarah Okafor>Half two, that's right. For once it's Teams and not me mumbling.</v>

00:03:44.460 --> 00:03:50.759
<v Sarah Okafor>Fiona Doyle, can you sort the training records for the new starters by end of day? No rush.</v>

00:03:51.460 --> 00:03:57.409
<v Fiona Doyle>Yes, I'll sort the training records for the new starters by end of day. No rush, understood.</v>

00:03:58.240 --> 00:04:02.090
<v David Whitfield>Full name. Sounds like you're being called into the head's office.</v>

00:04:04.189 --> 00:04:07.340
<v Fiona Doyle>Only if you've seen the state of my handwriting.</v>

00:04:09.169 --> 00:04:14.770
<v Sarah Okafor>And Fi, can you recalibrate the sensors on the chiller on three next Tuesday? Normal priority.</v>

00:04:15.849 --> 00:04:20.750
<v Fiona Doyle>Yeah, I'll recalibrate the sensors on the chiller on three next Tuesday, normal priority.</v>

00:04:22.250 --> 00:04:26.100
<v Aisha Begum>Sorry, did that come through as recalibrate? Lost half a word.</v>

00:04:26.519 --> 00:04:29.670
<v Fiona Doyle>Recalibrate, yeah. Not just looking at the displayed values.</v>

00:04:30.339 --> 00:04:31.740
<v Sarah Okafor>Right. Anything from process?</v>

00:04:32.389 --> 00:04:39.040
<v Aisha Begum>Yeah so, I'll organise the toolbox talk for the team on the 3rd. It matters but it's not urgent.</v>

00:04:40.990 --> 00:04:45.889
<v Sarah Okafor>Thanks, Aisha. Toolbox talk for the team on the 3rd, with you organising it.</v>

00:04:46.199 --> 00:04:50.750
<v Aisha Begum>That's right, I'll organise it. Not trying to squeeze it into this stand-up.</v>

00:04:53.000 --> 00:04:55.449
<v David Whitfield>Good, because my tea's already reaching ambient.</v>

00:04:56.079 --> 00:04:58.529
<v Hannah Price>There's your engineering unit for meeting duration.</v>

00:05:00.379 --> 00:05:10.180
<v David Whitfield>One thing still needing an owner: grease and check the bearings on the Air Compressor 1 tomorrow at 9:30. That's needing doing, not something that's already been done.</v>

00:05:11.959 --> 00:05:15.110
<v Sarah Okafor>Anyone able to take that bearing grease and check?</v>

00:05:15.949 --> 00:05:18.050
<v Tomasz Nowak>I can't take it on. Sorry.</v>

00:05:19.740 --> 00:05:24.290
<v David Whitfield>I'm flagging the gap, not volunteering. I can't commit to that slot either.</v>

00:05:26.720 --> 00:05:34.069
<v Sarah Okafor>Okay. Grease and check the bearings on the Air Compressor 1, tomorrow at 9:30, remains unowned. No pretending we've covered it.</v>

00:05:36.000 --> 00:05:40.199
<v Fiona Doyle>Yeah, nobody against that one. An open requirement, not an accepted job.</v>

00:05:42.279 --> 00:05:51.029
<v Tomasz Nowak>What I can take is the ordering. I'll order spare parts for the booster pump—whenever you get a chance sort of priority, no fixed date.</v>

00:05:51.420 --> 00:05:57.370
<v Sarah Okafor>Thanks, Tomasz Nowak. Ordering spare parts for the booster pump is yours, whenever you get a chance.</v>

00:05:58.199 --> 00:06:01.000
<v Tomasz Nowak>Yep, I'll do the ordering on that basis.</v>

00:06:01.589 --> 00:06:04.389
<v David Whitfield>Just spares, then, not a whole booster pump?</v>

00:06:04.870 --> 00:06:08.720
<v Tomasz Nowak>Just spare parts for the booster pump. Not a pump replacement.</v>

00:06:09.970 --> 00:06:16.269
<v Sarah Okafor>Right, that's clear. And the Fire Alarm Panel is still blocked, not quietly added to anyone else's list.</v>

00:06:16.850 --> 00:06:19.649
<v Tomasz Nowak>Exactly. Without that production window, there's no access.</v>

00:06:21.009 --> 00:06:26.610
<v Hannah Price>And those pressure dips stay as observations. I don't want anyone thinking I raised another repair.</v>

00:06:27.620 --> 00:06:32.870
<v Priya Raman>Same for the stretch wrapper. It recovered on its own; that's all I was reporting.</v>

00:06:34.689 --> 00:06:39.939
<v Sarah Okafor>Understood. We've kept the completed work, observations and the blocker separate from the agreed jobs.</v>

00:06:42.060 --> 00:06:45.209
<v Aisha Begum>Lovely. Back to watching the forecast get worse, then.</v>

00:06:46.949 --> 00:06:50.100
<v David Whitfield>Still reckon the pub window's the technically sound option.</v>

00:06:50.839 --> 00:06:54.339
<v Sarah Okafor>On that expert advice, we'll leave it there. Thanks, everyone.</v>
